{:edb/db-before-t 2057 :edb/db-after-t 2058 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535370 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:59:25.322Z" 13194139535370 true] [17592186161804 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/bba3d767c1001256f5bd4bd9bbf38059054dee21ff51714f1d3ed463f431a6c3.png)\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186161804 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${(c)/((1 + x + y)^{3}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x < y \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$." 13194139535370 false] [17592186161804 1145 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\lt y \\}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/9c823d67872095514c6392038ff43497512de656e28b26160465760df0faa4ea.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\iint_{D} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\int_{x}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{c}{(1+x+y)^3} \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{x}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{x}^{\\infty }(1+x+y)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}(1+x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=x}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[(1+x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=x}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }0-(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{c}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{2}(1+2x)^{-1}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{4}{[\\frac{1}{1+2x}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{4}[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{4}=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=4.\n$$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186161804 1145 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\lt y \\}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/f7c3d4d867e4cd8c83125f9b879c525e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0$, we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$. So the first condition is satisfied.\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have\n$\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\iint_{D} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\int_{x}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{c}{(1+x+y)^3} \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1$.\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{∞}[∫_{x}^{∞}(c)/((1 + x + y)^{3})dy]dx &= c∫_{0}^{∞}[∫_{x}^{∞}(1 + x + y)^{-3}dy]dx \\\\\n&= c∫_{0}^{∞}[- \\frac{1}{2}(1 + x + y)^{-2}]_{y = x}^{y = ∞}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}[(1 + x + y)^{-2}]_{y = x}^{y = ∞}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}0 - (1 + 2x)^{-2}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞} - (1 + 2x)^{-2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}(1 + 2x)^{-2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n> Then, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n> $\\frac{c}{2}∫_{0}^{∞}(1 + 2x)^{-2}dx|= \\frac{c}{2}[- \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 2x)^{-1}]_{0}^{∞}; =-\\frac{c}{4}[\\frac{1}{1 + 2x}]_{0}^{∞}; =-\\frac{c}{4}[0 - 1]; = \\frac{c}{4}$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{4}=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=4\n$$" 13194139535370 false] [17592186161805 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/f7aed2445b7b518afecd13a5979a873b8bfc17e8eb1ff8d41f4a0d9c912deaf4.png)\n\nFind $P(X > 1, Y < 1).$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186161805 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{6},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, 0 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind $P(X > 1, Y < 1)$." 13194139535370 false] [17592186161805 1145 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\\subset \\mathbb R^2$, then\n\n$$\nP\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\iint_A f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region\n\n$$\nD = [0,3]\\times [0,2]\n$$\n\nand the event\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{X > 1, Y < 1\\right\\}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to a subset of $D$, shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/a5d3eeb749188c014bfe84991e95ddc8.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 1, Y < 1) &= ∬_{A}f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}∬_{A}dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}Area(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (3 - 1) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535370 false] [17592186161805 1145 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ then\n\n$$\nP\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\iint_A f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D = [0,3]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A = \\left\\{ X > 1, Y < 1\\right\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/b1fc6548c909b9887c708c4c5dcce5b715d112defc7ce1fc639784bbaf55e111.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1,Y<1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186161810 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/a1b3940558e5a6d90d2911c18571eb91d63a4baa63bbe467fb8359c6fa5f44ea.png)\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186161810 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${c\\sin (x + y),\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < \\frac{π}{2}, 0 < y < \\frac{π}{2} \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$." 13194139535370 false] [17592186161810 1145 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\:  0\\lt x\\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},0\\lt y \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/ccb7cc52f8404dcca5d555d4fd81e6ae03e9689ccdd7c462fccbc2dc611db235.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}c\\sin (x+y) \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =c{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] \\\\  & =c[(1-0)-(0-1)] \\\\  & =c[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186161810 1145 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0\\lt x\\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},0\\lt y \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3052/Source/Images/f9bda8492314ef0374cbb01c5b669ef2.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0$, we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$. So the first condition is satisfied.\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have\n$\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}c\\sin (x+y) \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1$.\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n$∫_{0}^{π/2}[∫_{0}^{π/2}c\\sin (x + y)dy]dx|= c∫_{0}^{π/2}[∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (x + y)dy]dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}[- \\cos (x + y)]_{y = 0}^{y = π/2}dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}(-\\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2}) + \\cos x)dx; = c∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos x - \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2})dx$\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n$c∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos x - \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2})dx|= c[\\sin x - \\sin (x + \\frac{π}{2})]_{0}^{π/2}; = c[(\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) - \\sin π) - (\\sin (0) - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}))]; = c[(1 - 0) - (0 - 1)]; = c[1 - (-1)]; = 2c$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139535370 false] [17592186162004 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346262 13194139535370 true] [17592186162004 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346269 13194139535370 true] [17592186162004 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346276 13194139535370 true] [17592186162004 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346283 13194139535370 true] [17592186162005 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346290 13194139535370 true] [17592186162005 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346297 13194139535370 true] [17592186162005 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346304 13194139535370 true] [17592186162005 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346311 13194139535370 true] [17592186162005 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346318 13194139535370 true] [17592186162010 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346234 13194139535370 true] [17592186162010 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346241 13194139535370 true] [17592186162010 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346248 13194139535370 true] [17592186162010 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346255 13194139535370 true] [17592186162230 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535370 true] [17592186162230 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}cxy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186162230 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$f(x, y) = {cxy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le y < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$." 13194139535370 false] [17592186162230 1145 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y<1}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/01f3a8d99dd38ce2a728fe2cb4e4ab8de933ccd9ba27c87174779c428c303f48.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}-\\frac{c}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186162231 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535370 true] [17592186162231 1100 "Let $D = [0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]$. Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n1. $f(x, y) = {x + y,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n2. $g(x, y) = {-xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n3. $h(x, y) = {\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$" 13194139535370 false] [17592186162231 1100 "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n- $f(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $g(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $h(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186162231 1145 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{ }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}+2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,2)=-1\\cdot 2=-2≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346234 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346235 13194139535370 true] [17592186346234 1089 "q-110858" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346234 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535370 true] [17592186346234 1100 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346234 1113 #uuid "5ae02804-fba0-5c96-b940-671c232a4a2c" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346234 1145 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062830/assets/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[-c{e}^{-x-y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =[-c{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346236 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346237 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346238 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346239 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346240 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1041 "selection" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346239 13194139535370 true] [17592186346235 1110 #uuid "6b657716-d94a-5e07-8159-82a0683c8d63" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346236 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346236 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535370 true] [17592186346236 1111 #uuid "f564f0d3-7c7d-5d82-87a3-d13c59c706c6" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346237 1034 "2" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346237 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535370 true] [17592186346237 1111 #uuid "0d83fa88-d874-5f98-9227-e0ffb8353e79" 13194139535370 true] [17592186346238 1034